"""Curated English narrative for Syria's institutions and cabinet decisions.

Names, scripted conditions and native effect tooltips belong to their existing
providers. This layer replaces only description keys a caller already owns.
"""

OVERRIDES = {
    # Cabinet decisions: the complete approved set of fifty-six.
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_officer_bargain_desc": "The officer corps cannot be treated as a single loyal instrument while senior commanders dispute the government's direction. Negotiations can secure another measure of support and a temporary understanding, though no compact can replace a settlement of the divided command. Each round charges $2.000 billion to the treasury and can be financed through borrowing; at most three can build backing.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_cabinet_mediation_desc": "Bring the presidency, cabinet and party leadership to the same table. A settlement could preserve civilian direction, recognize the Regional Command's authority or prepare a negotiated transfer of office. Failure leaves the rivalry unresolved and damages public confidence; none of these men will surrender his position merely because talks have opened.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_corrective_vote_desc": "Submit the corrective programme to the command. A secure officer compact and cabinet assent can carry a transitional presidency into office. Attempting the change without that foundation risks ninety days of disrupted army organization, while withdrawing the proposal leaves room for further bargaining.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_succession_crisis_desc": "The president's incapacity has exposed the rival commands behind the apparent unity of the state. Convene a conference to choose collective stewardship, restore presidential authority or accept Rifaat's challenge. The outcome must address the actual struggle for command, rather than assume that an orderly succession has already been secured.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_guard_bargain_desc": "Rival guard commands have become centres of influence in their own right. Commit $0.096 billion to negotiations over a unified chain of command. An accepted settlement can resolve the confrontation; rejecting the proposed bargain risks ninety days of disruption within the army.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_front_offer_desc": "A National Front could bring Communist or Arab Socialist partners into the cabinet without erasing their organizations. Open talks with a legal partner that commands sufficient support, offering representation in return for agreed governing terms. The front will have substance only if the participating parties can accept the arrangement.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_citizenship_review_desc": "Commission staff must examine family records, hear appeals and correct the exclusions left by the 1962 census. Each funded stage costs $0.036 billion. Three completed stages will conclude the review, allowing equal-citizenship measures to rest on resolved cases rather than another unfulfilled declaration from Damascus.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_arab_belt_desc": "The proposed Jazira settlement brings land, citizenship and security into a single dangerous dispute. The cabinet can cancel discriminatory measures and fund a $0.072 billion tenure review, or impose a security settlement whose temporary control carries lasting exclusion and unrest. Suspending the scheme leaves the underlying choice unresolved.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_kurdish_coalition_desc": "Negotiate representation with Abdul Hamid Darwish, Salah Badr al-Din or both organizations. An appeals process and credible cultural guarantees must give the talks substance. The resulting compact belongs to this cabinet; its partners will expect a future government to negotiate its own commitments rather than inherit their consent automatically.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_enterprise_audit_desc": "Investigate an enterprise contract, its suppliers and the officials responsible for approving it. Each audit costs $0.036 billion and temporarily disrupts production while records are examined. Three completed investigations will provide the foundation for broader procurement reform, without pretending that one inspection can cleanse the entire state sector.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_tabqa_resettle_desc": "Families displaced by the Euphrates reservoir need homes, services and a viable future before the project can be commissioned responsibly. Commit $0.120 billion to the resettlement programme. Housing and welfare obligations will burden construction for 180 days, after which the completed work can support the next stage at Tabqa.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_irrigation_trial_desc": "Test water delivery, drainage and soil conditions before extending irrigation across the valley. Each trial stage costs $0.060 billion, and three separate stages must be completed. The results will support dependable irrigation and reduced rainfall vulnerability; trial plots alone are no substitute for the wider works still required.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_drought_desc": "A failed harvest threatens rural livelihoods and the country's food supply. Commit $0.072 billion to relief distribution, temporarily easing part of the agricultural loss while the crisis lasts. Assistance cannot restore the missing rains or turn an emergency into a permanent source of increased production.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_budget_review_desc": "Review how the state supports household consumption. Targeted relief can strengthen confidence while increasing social expenditure; subsidy restraint saves money but brings hardship and opposition. Choose a six-month response, accepting its political and financial consequences before another package can replace it.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_unrest_mediation_desc": "Local grievances have grown beyond what another order from the capital can settle. Spend $0.048 billion to bring civilian representatives into mediation. Meaningful guarantees can ease the crisis, but refusing them will leave unrest unresolved after the government has already paid for the attempt.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_security_response_desc": "Authorize emergency operations at a cost of $0.096 billion. Coercion may restore visible order temporarily, but it restricts intellectual life, increases administrative expenditure and invites foreign scrutiny. The aftermath lasts 180 days, and other governments will make their own decisions about how to respond.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_soviet_training_desc": "Ask Moscow to provide a military training mission. Soviet approval depends on available assistance capacity and a willingness to support the undertaking. Syria will pay $0.060 billion for hosting only if a new mission begins; an unanswered or declined request creates no training establishment and no hosting bill.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_development_credit_desc": "Request a $0.200 billion development loan from the Soviet Union. Acceptance requires a lender able and willing to supply the money, while Syria incurs an equal public debt. An attractive project does not make the advance free, and opening negotiations does not guarantee that Moscow will finance it.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_friendship_treaty_desc": "Propose a friendship and cooperation treaty to Moscow, setting out a more durable basis for consultation. The Soviet government may accept or decline the terms. An accepted agreement strengthens the relationship, while further aid, loans and joint undertakings remain subjects for their own negotiations.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_gulf_project_desc": "Present a civil development project to a Gulf partner. The proposed $0.200 billion contribution must come from a government prepared to approve and finance it. Only an accepted, funded agreement can produce the selected project; diplomatic interest and a pending offer do not pay the construction bill.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_pipeline_desc": "Invite Iraq to agree operating rights and transit payments for the pipeline through Syria. An accepted arrangement can support regular revenue and practical cooperation, but the route earns nothing while negotiations remain unsettled. Payment difficulties or a diplomatic rupture can bring the operation to an end.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_federation_desc": "Offer a federal cooperation agreement to an Arab partner, identifying a basis for closer consultation without assuming that sovereignty has already been surrendered. The other government must decide whether to accept. A signed agreement opens cooperation; any eventual political union would require a separate and explicit settlement.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_golan_desc": "Send Israel a package for discussions on disengagement, security and the occupied Golan. Acceptance would establish a negotiating channel, leaving the actual terms to the governments concerned. Neither the proposal nor a willingness to talk transfers territory or settles the war on behalf of other belligerents.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_joint_plan_desc": "Offer Egypt 180 days of staff cooperation. If Cairo accepts, Syria will contribute 0.25% of its current GDP and maintain the additional planning establishment. The mission can improve coordination without committing either government to an automatic offensive or duplicating an existing Egyptian planning arrangement.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_lebanon_desc": "Offer Lebanon a limited security assistance mission under a revocable invitation. The Lebanese government must accept before Syria can consider deployment, and the operation will require separate authorization and funding. An invitation is a negotiated responsibility, not a transfer of Lebanese sovereignty or control of its forces.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_regional_withdrawal_desc": "Withdraw the accepted Lebanese assistance mission and close its invitation. The temporary military benefits and the personnel and fuel commitments will end together. Syria will retain the knowledge already acquired and remain responsible for its public debts, while ending this particular foreign obligation.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_hard_currency_desc": "Foreign exchange shortages have brought competing demands to the cabinet. Consider six months of subsidy restraint or import compression, accepting the hardship each causes, or seek a negotiated development loan which adds debt if approved. Deferring the choice leaves the crisis awaiting a funded response.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_iran_oil_desc": "Approach the post-revolutionary Iranian government for a one-year supply and credit arrangement. Accepted terms provide a $0.100 billion advance with equal Syrian debt and a temporary interest premium. Continued supply cooperation depends on the political relationship; its collapse will not erase money Syria has already borrowed.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_campaign_western_desc": "Choose a registered constitutional party for a focused organizing campaign among voters and civic networks. Its supporters must persuade Syrians who remember both parliamentary bargaining and the crises that preceded military rule. The campaign builds that party's support; office must still be won through the country's political institutions.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_campaign_emerging_desc": "Choose a legal socialist organization and mobilize its supporters around a distinct programme. Workers, public employees and rural activists do not all agree on what socialism requires of the state. This campaign strengthens the chosen party's voice without settling those arguments or placing it automatically in government.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_campaign_nonaligned_desc": "Campaign for a registered independent Arab party, bringing its particular vision of unity, society and national independence before the public. Shared language about Arab interests will not remove political differences. The chosen organization can gain support while remaining responsible for winning authority through the existing political process.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_campaign_nationalist_desc": "Support a legal nationalist organization through a focused political campaign. Its activists must explain why their answer to Syria's weakness deserves confidence over the competing programmes of officers and rival parties. Organizing builds support for that choice; it does not itself confer the right to govern.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_reopen_registers_desc": "Reverse the refusal to reconsider the citizenship registers. Reopening the question costs $0.180 billion and brings 180 days of political uncertainty as old decisions are challenged. The same substantive appeals process must then resolve the remaining cases; accepting review is the beginning of redress, not its completion.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_national_pact_desc": "Invite the cabinet and political leadership to negotiate lawful opposition and competitive elections. Accepting the pact will end the monopoly of one-party government and bring 180 days of difficult adjustment. Refusal preserves the restrictions, while agreement leaves future governments to be determined by political support and elections.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_governing_mandate_desc": "Clarify whether the governing programme follows Nureddin al-Atassi's civilian direction or Salah Jadid's dominant Regional Command. The choice concerns the authority behind policy while Atassi remains the formal president. Any later transfer of the presidency must be achieved through a separate and genuine settlement.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_military_transfer_desc": "Negotiate a constitutional transfer from military rule to civilian government, including legal parties and competitive elections. The army will face a period of uncertainty as its political authority recedes. Accepting the transfer establishes a route to civilian power without selecting the electorate's eventual governing party in advance.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_coalition_western_desc": "Negotiate cabinet terms with a legal constitutional partner that has sufficient public support. Conservatives and civic reformers may agree on parliamentary institutions while disputing social and economic policy. An accepted bargain brings the partner into the coalition, leaving the governing party responsible for maintaining the agreement.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_coalition_emerging_desc": "Seek a governing agreement with a legal socialist partner that commands sufficient support. Cabinet representation can give cooperation practical meaning, provided the parties accept common responsibilities without pretending their programmes are identical. The resulting coalition adds a partner rather than replacing the party already leading the government.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_coalition_nonaligned_desc": "Discuss cabinet representation with a legal independent Arab partner that has a sufficient base of support. A successful agreement must reconcile its priorities with those of the governing party. Coalition membership follows accepted terms, while each organization retains its identity and responsibility toward its supporters.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_coalition_nationalist_desc": "Invite a sufficiently supported and legal nationalist party into coalition negotiations. Agreement will require practical compromises among organizations with competing claims to national authority. An accepted partner joins the cabinet's coalition; it does not acquire an automatic right to replace the current government or dictate its programme.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_funded_project_desc": "Put forward a specific development undertaking with a partner and a real source of finance. The proposed $0.200 billion contribution will be paid only after acceptance, supporting one agreed project. The offer can expire without construction if the other government decides not to commit its resources.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_iraq_transit_desc": "Open formal pipeline negotiations with Baghdad over operating rights, transit payments and the conditions needed to keep the route working. Until the two governments accept an arrangement, Syria cannot count on transit income. An operating contract will remain dependent on payment and a workable bilateral relationship.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_gulf_finance_desc": "Approach a Gulf government with a concrete Syrian development proposal. A prospective partner must judge the project and approve the use of its own treasury. The negotiation offers a route to financed construction, while preserving the other government's right to decline or let the proposal lapse.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_authorize_mission_desc": "Act on Lebanon's accepted invitation and authorize the limited assistance mission. Syria must pay $0.080 billion in setup costs and support personnel and fuel commitments for up to 180 days. The Lebanese government can revoke its invitation, and the mission does not assume authority over its territory or armed forces.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_war_appropriation_desc": "Approve a one-time appropriation equal to 0.75% of Syria's current GDP for the Golan operations programme. The funding remains available for one year while the cabinet decides whether to authorize preparations. It does not itself mobilize the reserves or replace the separate political mandate required for active operations.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_war_reserves_desc": "Call up the prepared reserve establishment at a one-time cost of 0.50% of current GDP. For 120 days, stronger readiness will improve organization while personnel expenditure rises and factory output falls under the additional demands. The advantages and burdens end together when the campaign commitment lapses.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_war_stockpiles_desc": "Commit 0.50% of current GDP to forward supply preparation. For 120 days, improved distribution will reduce supply and army fuel consumption while requiring additional personnel expenditure. The programme organizes the resources already available to the army rather than promising equipment or stores that Syria has not acquired.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_war_egypt_desc": "Invite Egypt to coordinate operations through a defined staff mission. If Cairo accepts, Syria will pay 0.25% of current GDP and support the additional bureaucracy for 180 days. Existing Egyptian coalition coordination takes precedence, avoiding a second payment for overlapping work; no invitation can commit Egypt to war without its own decision.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_war_medical_desc": "Spend 0.25% of current GDP to prepare forward medical services for 120 days. Better treatment and evacuation arrangements can improve organization and confidence, while raising healthcare expenditure. The medical establishment is part of the campaign's real cost and will stand down with its associated operational commitment.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_war_window_desc": "Commit 0.50% of current GDP to the operational approach already prepared by the staff. Authorize either a sixty-day short offensive or 120 days of sustained pressure, with the corresponding supply and personnel burdens. A campaign receives one such window; the decision leaves any declaration of war to the government.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_war_goal_armistice_desc": "While at peace and the Golan remains under Israeli occupation, commit 0.25% of current GDP to authorize a renewed war goal lasting one year. The goal keeps the military option available after an armistice. It does not declare war, and cannot be purchased again while an equivalent goal already exists.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_war_warning_desc": "Notify Israel that recovery of the occupied Golan is Syria's stated political objective. The Israeli government may accept a negotiating channel or reject the position. The notice neither requests permission to act nor assumes consent: any territorial agreement or peace settlement still requires explicit negotiations.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_war_mediation_desc": "Request talks on disengagement, separation of forces and security around the Golan. Israel's acceptance would open a negotiating channel, while final peace terms remain the responsibility of the actual belligerents and coalition leaders. Silence or refusal cannot be treated as agreement on their behalf.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_war_credit_desc": "Arrange a $0.100 billion emergency advance with an equal increase in public debt. The bridge loan brings a one-year interest premium and weakens confidence, even if it meets an immediate campaign need. Only one can be taken for this campaign, and demobilization will not cancel its principal.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_war_demobilize_desc": "Begin standing down the campaign establishment, ending temporary reserve, supply, medical and planning advantages together with their operating burdens. The army retains professional skills it has genuinely learned. Borrowed money remains public debt, and demobilization provides no refund for resources already spent preparing the campaign.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_war_reconvene_desc": "Recall the operational cabinet to consider preparations previously held in reserve. An already-paid appropriation can still support authorization while it remains valid, without another funding charge. The government must decide whether the Golan objective justifies activating the mandate before the appropriation expires.",

    # The starting institutions and legacies of January 1970.
    "COLDWAR_SYR_divided_command_desc": "Nureddin al-Atassi holds the presidency, Salah Jadid dominates the party apparatus and Hafez al-Assad commands the Defence Ministry. Their competing sources of authority obstruct coherent government and weaken military organization. A real settlement between civilian institutions, the party and the officer corps is needed to end the divided command.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_emergency_state_desc": "Emergency rule has endured since the Ba'ath seized power in 1963. Exceptional powers help the authorities preserve order, but restrict public debate and leave political initiative concentrated in institutions suspicious of independent thought. Constitutional change must resolve those restrictions if the republic is to move beyond a permanent state of exception.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_legacy_1967_desc": "The defeat of 1967 exposed grave weaknesses in command and left the Golan under Israeli occupation. Anger sustains the determination to confront Israel, while the army carries the institutional damage of its collapse. Military reform can repair organization; recovering territory remains a separate political and military question.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_uneven_services_desc": "National development has reached Syria's cities and countryside unevenly. Shortages of technical skills limit the use of new knowledge, while incomplete networks and services slow construction. Education and dependable infrastructure must each address their own part of a weakness which no single public project can remove.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_state_sector_strain_desc": "Land reform and nationalization have transformed ownership and widened the state's responsibilities. Their social promise is burdened by confused administration, weak enterprise coordination and rural institutions unable to meet every demand. Productive agrarian arrangements and professional economic administration are both needed to make the transformation sustainable.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_rain_sensitive_farms_desc": "Much of Syrian agriculture still depends on uncertain rainfall. The unfinished Euphrates works offer the prospect of more reliable water, but a dam cannot irrigate fields by itself. Working distribution, drainage and protection against salinity must follow if rural livelihoods are to become less vulnerable to the weather.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_citizenship_exclusion_desc": "The exceptional census of 1962 left many Kurdish families in the northeast without recognized citizenship. Exclusion from ordinary rights and public institutions has produced a lasting grievance between communities and the state. A credible appeals process and completed review are necessary to turn assurances of equal citizenship into everyday reality.",

    # Temporary burdens, interventions and consequences of domestic policy.
    "COLDWAR_SYR_politics_command_disruption_desc": "A contested transfer of authority has left officers uncertain whose instructions will prevail. Rival loyalties and interrupted coordination weaken the army's organization until a dependable chain of command can reassert itself. Political victory at the top has not yet produced cohesion within the ranks.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_politics_officer_compact_desc": "A temporary understanding with influential officers has eased immediate uncertainty. The compromise strengthens confidence while it lasts, but personal assurances are not a permanent institutional settlement. The government must use the breathing space to resolve the struggle between the party, cabinet and armed forces.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_politics_transition_burden_desc": "Legal opposition and competitive politics are replacing the familiar discipline of one-party rule. Expectations, rival organizations and unresolved guarantees make this a difficult adjustment, reducing the government's political freedom and public confidence while the new constitutional arrangements establish themselves.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_politics_exclusion_desc": "The security settlement has reinforced exclusion in the Jazira rather than resolved the dispute over land and citizenship. Grievances undermine confidence in the state and consume political authority. Reconciliation requires substantive review and redress, rather than a change in the language used to defend the policy.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_politics_security_control_desc": "Security authorities are imposing the government's chosen settlement through an expanded administrative presence. Visible control may strengthen order for a time, but supervision and enforcement increase bureaucracy costs while the underlying questions of land, representation and citizenship remain to be answered.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_politics_enterprise_audit_desc": "Investigators are examining procurement records, supplier arrangements and the conduct of enterprise officials. The inquiry disrupts normal production while evidence is secured and decisions are reviewed. That temporary loss is part of the cost of learning how public contracts have actually been administered.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_politics_resettlement_burden_desc": "Households displaced by the Euphrates reservoir need replacement homes and access to services. Meeting those obligations draws resources away from other construction and increases social expenditure. The reservoir's promise cannot justify leaving the people affected by its creation without a viable future.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_politics_crop_crisis_desc": "Failed harvests have reduced agricultural output and placed rural incomes under pressure. Relief can ease some of the immediate loss, but the missing crop exposes the country's dependence on rainfall and the unfinished work of building a more resilient agricultural economy.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_politics_drought_relief_desc": "Relief teams are distributing assistance to districts suffering from failed harvests. Their work offsets part of the agricultural loss while the crisis persists, helping cultivators keep their farms functioning. It cannot replace favourable weather or remove the longer-term need for sound water and soil management.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_politics_targeted_relief_desc": "Additional support is reaching households judged most vulnerable to rising costs and lost income. The programme strengthens confidence in public protection, while requiring greater social expenditure. Its temporary character leaves the government responsible for deciding how support will be financed when the present relief period ends.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_politics_restraint_desc": "The government is restraining subsidy commitments to reduce social expenditure. Savings reach the budget before households can adjust to the loss of familiar protection, weakening confidence and giving critics a visible grievance. Financial relief for the state comes with an immediate political cost.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_politics_unrest_desc": "Unresolved grievances have spread beyond isolated local complaints. Distrust weakens public confidence and disrupts industrial activity, while the government faces demands it can no longer dismiss through routine administration. Mediation or coercion will carry very different costs, and neither should be mistaken for an effortless settlement.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_politics_mediation_guarantees_desc": "Civilian mediation has produced guarantees that public institutions must now honour. Hearings, supervision and continued contact require additional administration, but these obligations give the agreement meaning beyond the meeting room. Confidence will depend on whether local communities see the promised protections applied in practice.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_politics_security_aftermath_desc": "Emergency operations have strengthened visible control while restricting intellectual activity and placing new demands on public administration. The government's greater capacity to impose order is accompanied by the financial and institutional burden of enforcement, alongside questions about the consequences for civilians and Syria's foreign relationships.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_politics_register_reversal_desc": "Reopening citizenship cases challenges decisions the authorities previously defended as final. Old assumptions, competing claims and the prospect of redress create a difficult period of uncertainty. The disruption will endure while the administration begins the substantive work needed to restore confidence in the review.",

    # Historically appropriate descriptions for the ten active Syrian parties.
    "COLDWAR_SYR_party_1_desc": "The constitutional conservative tradition draws upon the parliamentary republic, commercial networks and civilian statesmen displaced by military rule. Its supporters seek restored legality and representative institutions, while differing over how much of the social and economic transformation of the 1960s should be retained.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_party_2_desc": "Civic liberals bring lawyers, professional circles and advocates of representative government into a common political current. They seek public accountability and civil freedoms, but must build durable institutions in a country where factional conflict and military intervention have repeatedly overwhelmed the promises of constitutional life.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_party_3_desc": "The independent socialist opposition seeks a Marxist political life beyond the discipline of the dominant party. Its emerging Political Bureau under Riyad al-Turk combines social demands with a growing concern for political freedom, placing it in difficult relations with both the Ba'ath authorities and the established Communist leadership.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_party_4_desc": "The Syrian Communist Party has deep organizing experience and a leadership closely associated with Khalid Bakdash. Cooperation with the Ba'ath offers influence but sharpens arguments over independence and democracy. Later factional divisions will test whether participation in government serves the movement's social programme or subordinates it.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_party_7_desc": "The Arab Socialist Ba'ath Party governs through a fusion of revolutionary institutions, the Regional Command and the armed forces. Arab unity and socialism supply its public language, while rival civilian and military centres struggle over who has the authority to define the party's direction.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_party_12_desc": "The Syrian Muslim Brotherhood draws on religious, urban and social networks that contest the secular state's claim to political authority. Its leaders differ over organization and political accommodation. A lawful governing route requires a settlement broad enough to protect the republic's diverse communities and sustain public institutions.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_party_13_desc": "The Arab Socialist Union carries the Nasserist tradition of Arab unity, social reform and an enduring connection to Cairo. Its Syrian leaders must decide how to pursue that ideal while preserving an independent voice within domestic politics and negotiating the powerful institutions surrounding the Ba'ath state.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_party_20_desc": "The Syrian Social Nationalist Party advocates a wider Syrian nation distinct from the Ba'ath's pan-Arab vision. Its disciplined organization brings a long history of activism and confrontation, while any return to governing influence must confront the practical demands of institutions, regional relations and accommodation with rival political currents.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_party_21_desc": "Iraqi-aligned Ba'athists preserve networks and ambitions displaced by the struggle within Syria's ruling movement. Amin al-Hafiz offers an exile's claim to restoration, but recovering power would not erase the rivalry between Damascus and Baghdad or settle which country's leadership should define the reunited party's direction.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_party_22_desc": "A provisional military council claims authority from the armed forces and the need to preserve national order. Its officers must choose whether emergency stewardship becomes an enduring political system or gives way to a negotiated civilian settlement, while keeping the army coherent enough to fulfil its defence responsibilities.",

    # Additional leadership and reconciliation decisions generated by politics.py.
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_choose_conservative_leader_desc": "The governing constitutional conservatives must choose their representative. Nazim al-Qudsi and Ma'ruf al-Dawalibi bring different political records and priorities to the restored civilian tradition. Endorsing either statesman places his own governing programme before the cabinet without settling the policies that it must still carry out.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_choose_communist_leader_desc": "The governing Communists must decide whether Khalid Bakdash's established leadership or Yusuf Faisal's organized faction should direct their programme. Faisal's alternative becomes available only after his faction has formed. The decision concerns leadership within the party, alongside the wider arguments over its relationship with the Ba'ath.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_choose_nasserist_leader_desc": "Choose who will lead the governing Arab Socialist Union. Jamal al-Atassi offers one political direction, while Safwan al-Qudsi's later leadership must first build its own organization. The choice determines whose programme the government can pursue without assuming that either man has already secured every commitment of his supporters.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_choose_brotherhood_leader_desc": "A governing Brotherhood must settle its own leadership as well as its relationship with the republic. Issam al-Attar and Adnan Saadeddin offer distinct political programmes; Saadeddin can be considered after his leadership organization has formed. The resulting choice still leaves substantive policy decisions to the cabinet.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_form_political_bureau_desc": "The Communist split of 1973 opens space for Riyad al-Turk's Political Bureau to organize an independent socialist current. Establishing it gives the movement a political foundation, while lawful participation depends on an accepted national pact and governing authority must still be won through public support.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_form_saadeddin_leadership_desc": "From 1975, Adnan Saadeddin's supporters can establish the leadership organization needed to offer their own direction for the Brotherhood. Building that network prepares a political alternative; a governing Brotherhood must still make a separate choice about who will lead it.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_form_safwan_leadership_desc": "From 1983, organize the later Arab Socialist leadership associated with Safwan al-Qudsi. Its establishment gives the movement another political direction to consider, while a government led by the Arab Socialist Union must still decide whether to endorse him as its leader.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_form_faisal_faction_desc": "The Communist division of 1986 allows Yusuf Faisal's supporters to organize a distinct faction. Its formation brings their alternative into the party's leadership debate, while replacing the leader of a governing Communist cabinet remains a separate decision rather than an automatic consequence of the split.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_withdraw_rights_coalition_desc": "End the cabinet's representation compact with the Kurdish rights organizations. Their associated governing programmes will cease to guide the administration, while the policies already developed remain available if a future government negotiates a valid new agreement. Ending representation does not itself establish a new citizenship settlement.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_reconvene_mediation_desc": "Previous mediation failed to resolve the struggle between the cabinet, party and armed forces. Reopen the talks and give the rivals another opportunity to agree who can issue binding orders. The offices and underlying conflict remain as they are until an actual settlement is accepted.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_decision_reopen_pact_talks_desc": "Return to constitutional negotiations after the earlier proposal was rejected. Legal opposition and competitive elections require a genuine agreement, with the responsibilities and uncertainty it brings. Reopening talks gives the cabinet another choice without assuming that the institutions resisting reform have already accepted its terms.",

    # Political event prose: preserve every option and scripted consequence.
    "COLDWAR_SYR_POL.2.d": "The corrective programme has reached the command. Hafez al-Assad's supporters want an end to the rivalry paralysing government, but a transition will endure only with sufficient officer backing and cabinet assent. A secure agreement can establish an interim presidency; proceeding without it risks disruption within the army, while withdrawal would return the question to negotiation.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_POL.3.d": "Nureddin al-Atassi occupies the presidency, while Salah Jadid exercises decisive influence through the Regional Command. The party must now clarify whose direction will guide policy. Endorsing Jadid's mandate acknowledges the authority of the party apparatus without making him president; endorsing Atassi strengthens the civilian policy mandate while preserving the existing office.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_POL.4.d": "Opposition organizations want a place in political life protected by law, alongside competitive elections and guarantees for peaceful activity. Accepting their terms would end the government's exclusive claim to organize the republic, beginning a difficult period of adjustment. Refusing the pact preserves one-party rule and leaves the emergency restrictions in force.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_POL.20.d": "A constitutional campaign needs an organization whose programme voters can judge. Choose a legal party to support through meetings, professional networks and local organizing. Its gains will strengthen its position in public politics, while the question of government remains for the country's representative institutions to decide.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_POL.21.d": "Socialist organizers disagree over which movement can best defend workers, farmers and the republic's independence. Direct the campaign toward one legal party and build its public support. The choice gives that organization's programme a stronger voice without handing it office or dissolving its rivals.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_POL.22.d": "Choose a legal independent Arab party for the organizing campaign. Its supporters must explain how national independence and Arab commitments should shape everyday government, rather than rely on a common vocabulary to conceal their differences. Campaigning can strengthen that party's public base while leaving authority to the political process.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_POL.23.d": "Several movements claim to offer the national discipline Syria needs, but their programmes are far from interchangeable. Choose a legal party to support through a focused campaign. The resulting gain strengthens its position among the public without authorizing it to take office by the campaign's decision alone.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_POL.24.d": "The cabinet can seek a constitutional partner with at least five percent public support. Representation must be negotiated around accepted terms, while the governing party remains responsible for its own programme. A successful bargain adds a coalition member; if the parties cannot agree, no membership will be granted.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_POL.25.d": "A socialist partner with at least five percent support may join the cabinet's coalition if its organization is legal and the proposed terms are accepted. Representation can give cooperation practical meaning, but disagreements remain to be negotiated. The governing party keeps its office and gains a partner rather than a replacement.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_POL.26.d": "The cabinet is considering representation for a legal independent Arab partner with at least five percent support. Its participation would bring another programme and another constituency into government. An accepted agreement grants coalition membership, while failure to settle the terms leaves the prospective partner outside.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_POL.27.d": "A nationalist partner must have a legal organization and at least five percent support before it can enter these cabinet negotiations. An accepted bargain brings it into the governing coalition without transferring the premiership or dissolving the parties' differences. The cabinet can also conclude that no workable agreement is available.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_POL.30.d": "The restored constitutional tradition has more than one statesman able to claim its leadership. Nazim al-Qudsi and Ma'ruf al-Dawalibi bring different records, alliances and priorities to the task. The governing conservatives must choose who will represent their programme through the difficult work of rebuilding civilian authority.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_POL.31.d": "The Communists must decide who should direct their governing programme. Khalid Bakdash represents the party's established leadership, while Yusuf Faisal's later faction offers a separate course once its organization has formed. The choice will shape the party's approach to authority, cooperation and the institutions through which it hopes to advance socialism.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_POL.32.d": "The Arab Socialist Union must choose a leader capable of translating its Nasserist commitments into Syrian government. Jamal al-Atassi offers one direction, while Safwan al-Qudsi's later organization can offer another after it has been established. Arab unity does not remove the need to settle who speaks for the movement at home.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_POL.33.d": "The Brotherhood's governing organization faces a choice of leadership and political direction. Issam al-Attar can be endorsed now; Adnan Saadeddin can be considered after his leadership organization has formed. Their supporters must decide whose programme can guide the movement while answering the responsibilities of governing a diverse republic.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_POL.34.d": "The military cabinet can accept a constitution permitting legal parties and competitive elections, opening the way to civilian government. Such a transfer will unsettle commands accustomed to political authority, and the eventual governing party will depend on the electorate's support. Postponement preserves military rule and leaves the constitutional question unresolved.",

    "COLDWAR_SYR_politics_category_desc": "Resolve the rivalry within the Ba'ath state, negotiate political representation and carry reforms into the provinces. Citizenship appeals, enterprise investigations and Euphrates works require time and funding; talks with officers and civilian parties will demand compromises which a cabinet declaration cannot secure on its own.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_foreign_dossier_desc": "Manage Syria's foreign commitments and preparations around the occupied Golan. Agreements need willing partners, while operations require a political mandate, appropriations and support services. Consider what the country can sustain before turning a diplomatic promise or a military plan into an active obligation.",
    "COLDWAR_SYR_counterparty_dossier_desc": "Review the assistance and transit commitments agreed with Damascus. Cooperation remains subject to this government's consent and the continued fulfilment of the terms. An invitation or operating agreement can be ended when it no longer serves the interests for which it was accepted.",
}


def improve(loc):
    """Polish descriptions already registered by the calling provider, in place."""
    for key in loc.keys() & OVERRIDES.keys():
        loc[key] = OVERRIDES[key]
    return loc


def override(mapping):
    """Compatibility spelling for callers treating this as an override layer."""
    return improve(mapping)
